Package: esidx
Version: @VERSION@
Section: utils
Priority: optional
Architecture: @ARCH@
Depends: libc6 (>= 2.34)
Maintainer: @MAINTAINER@
Installed-Size: @SIZE@
Description: ext4 search index engine with an Everything (ETP) server
 esidx walks one or more directory trees into a snapshot, answers Everything's search
 language (ext:, dc:, dm:, size:, parent:, path:, wildcards, boolean and grouping) over the
 ETP protocol, and reconciles that snapshot from kernel filesystem events so it stays
 current without a periodic rescan.
 .
 The locations are named in /etc/esidx/roots, one absolute path per line. A snapshot may
 cover several of them: parent:"" and root: then mean the union of their top levels, which
 is what a client showing one volume with several trees underneath has to mean. A location
 may not appear twice or inside another -- that would give every file below it two rows,
 which no downstream step can undo -- and the build refuses such a set before walking
 anything.
 .
 The event source is fanotify, which needs CAP_SYS_ADMIN. In this deployment one process
 holds it: the unit starts esidx as root, the binary opens the fanotify group, gives the
 capability back before it opens the listener, and keeps only CAP_DAC_READ_SEARCH -- the
 one thing that turns an event into a path. What the process is allowed to hold is stated
 in the unit's CapabilityBoundingSet, so the ceiling is a property of the install rather
 than a promise in a comment. A mark covers one mount, so an index whose locations are on
 different mounts gets one mark each; a tree with a mount point *inside* it is repaired by
 the periodic sweep rather than by events.
 .
 The two-process form is still shipped: sfa-server is installed as well, a root proxy that
 hands absolute paths over a group-owned socket to an unprivileged esidx. Upgrading from a
 package that ran it retires the two units it used. In that form the locations have to be
 named twice -- the proxy's --prefix and what esidx accepts -- because a proxy holds one
 mark; the embedded form above is what avoids it.
 .
 This package installs one systemd unit, esidx.service, and the two files it is configured
 by: /etc/esidx/roots (the locations) and /etc/default/esidx (everything else). It enables
 the unit but does not start it: the server needs a snapshot, and building the first one
 scans every tree named, which does not belong in a maintainer script. The install output
 says the two commands.</new_string>
